  "analysis": {
    "key_findings": [
      "All three RC16 models score 4/9. 152b (AR flow) reference scored 5/9.",
      "153a has best conditionality (turb/calm=1.47) but worst CI coverage (worst_cell=0.10). Too narrow spreads.",
      "154b has best CI coverage (worst_cell=0.54, mean=0.76) but destroys conditionality (turb/calm=0.96). Residual is unconditional so it adds uniform spread.",
      "154c is between 154b and 153a on CI (worst_cell=0.46, mean=0.64) and also loses conditionality (turb/calm=0.92).",
      "All three have catastrophic explosion_rate in 154b/154c (>50%) because residual noise pushes samples outside [0.001, 0.99]. 153a is clean (0.02%).",
      "KS daily is best for 153a (23/25), slightly degraded for 154b/154c (18-19/25). KS levels is poor for all (<3/25).",
      "Cointegration improves with residual FM: 153a=0.44, 154b=0.66, 154c=0.56. Both 154s pass S6.",
      "Cross-cell correlation is best for 154c (rank_ratio=1.11, closest to 1.0). 153a slightly under-ranks (0.85), 154b over-ranks (1.29).",
      "None pass S2 (CI), S7 (Regime), or S8 (Distributional). The core CI bottleneck remains.",
      "152b (AR flow) remains superior: passes S1, better KS (20/25 daily, 9/25 levels), comparable CI."
    ],
    "tradeoff_summary": "Residual FM (154b/154c) trades conditionality for coverage. The unconditional residual in 154b adds more spread but is regime-blind. Conditioning the residual (154c) does NOT restore conditionality -- it actually worsens it (0.92 vs 0.96), suggesting the conditional FM learned to be anti-conditional or the conditioning signal is too weak at this model size."
  }
